speechtext:
I was quite certain that my right hon. friend would say "hear, hear" to that, because I happen to know, not from what he has said publicly ibut, if he will permit me, from what he has said privately, that he concurs in the statement that that is the only basis upon which sound accounting in this country can be maintained. No contract or obligation shall be valid unless it bears upon its face a stamp of some kind indicating that it has been entered by the comptroller of the treasury. If I or any minister desires to know what the position is, there is a record which will indicate the financial position from day to day.
The comptroller acts as a check upon the spending departments, the auditor general acts as a check upon him, because the cancelled cheques immediately flow into the joint control of the auditor general and the Minister of Finance. In addition, the auditor general is now given complete freedom in the examination of the vouchers, records and banking accounts, and may also place his officers in any department in order that he may be fully informed during the course of his audit. At the present time, strange as it may sound, the freedom of investigation by the auditor general into many accounts is very limited. It is only with the *permission of the Minister of Finance or the treasury board that he makes an audit of the following accounts:
(a)	the accounts of all receipts of revenues forming the consolidated revenue fund of Canada;
(b)	the accounts current with several banks and financial agents of Canada;
(c)	the accounts relating to the issue or reduction of loans;
(d) the accounts writh the several Indian tribes, known as the Indian fund;
(e)	the accounts with the several provinces forming the Dominion of Canada;
(f) the accounts with the government of Great Britain;
fg) the receipt, expenditure, sale, transfer or delivery of any securities, stamps, Canadian or other government stock or annuities, provisions or stores; and
(h) public accounts which do not relate directly to the receipts or expenditure of the Dominion of Canada.
We propose to change that. While in practice the auditor general has been encouraged to audit many of these accounts, it is not fitting that such audits should be by invitation. Therefore, the new sections make it obligatory in some cases, discretionary with the auditor general in others, that regular audits be made, and to provide for the future, the treasury board is to be vested with power to enlarge the list of activities which the auditor general shall report upon to parliament. In this way it is hoped that full scope * may be given to the auditor general to perform the duty laid upon him by the section which creates his office.
We have endeavoured through the revision of old and the addition of new sections to meet the present day requirements of a public service functioning throughout an area of over 3.000 miles from coast to coast, and at the same time to provide means whereby the growth of the country may be met without the invention of subterfuges to circumvent statutory provisions which, if too narrowly drawn, would defeat the purpose for which they were designed.
It is proposed that the bill shall come into general effect on April 1, next, but provision is made whereby the change may be made gradually throughout the balance of the present fiscal year. In other words, we shall not endeavour by any revolutionary act to change the existing system but rather, by applying the principles I have indicated, bring all the departments under the provisions of the statute.
I may say that the departments of the public service have been circularized as of June 15, for the number of employees engaged on expenditure accounting, both at Ottawa and outside of Ottawa. The record shows that there are 898 people thus engaged at Ottawa, and 411 outside of Ottawa, and that these are in receipt of salaries amounting to $2,211,211. I have before me a statement showing the number of employees engaged in accounting in the various departments. The employees in the auditor general's department have increased to 193, with 18 temporary employees, making a total of 211 receiving annual salaries amounting to $388360.
It is proposed that the drafts made upon the public treasury to liquidate obligations created shall be in the form used so largely in other countries, that is, drawn upon the Receiver General of Canada and made payable at all banks in the country, as provided b}' the Bank Act, without charge.
I think those are the general provisions of this bill, of which I now move the second reading.
At six o'clock the house took recess.

